    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is It a Good Offer?\n\n**Yes, the headline numbers are strong for an L6 PM, but the structure has nuances you must account for.**\n\n* **Year 1 & Year 2 upside is significant:** Google standard new-hire GSU grants are front-loaded (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** vesting monthly):\n  * **Year 1 Total Comp at Google:**\n    * Base: $235,000\n    * Bonus (20% target): $47,000\n    * Equity (at 33%): ~$356,400\n    * **Year 1 TC:** **~$638,400** (vs. your ~$476k\u2013$526k at Meta)\n* **The \"Year 3 & 4 Cliff\":** Because Google front-loads new-hire equity, your equity from this initial grant drops sharply in Year 3 ($237,600) and Year 4 ($129,600). Google relies on annual refreshers to backfill this dip, but refreshers are performance-dependent and vest over multiple years.\n* **The Base Pay Cut:** Your base would drop from **$240k to $235k**, despite the posted salary band capping at **$271k**.\n* **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Google routinely uses sign-on bonuses to bridge transitions and buy out near-term unvested equity/bonuses.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**You should definitely negotiate.** \n\nYou are in the strongest possible position:\n1. You were **sourced passively** (they reached out to you).\n2. You are **happy at Meta** and have zero urgency to leave.\n3. You are taking on role/team risk by leaving an IC6 position where you already have established equity and performance standing.\n\nGoogle will not pull an offer because an L6 candidate professionally negotiates with clear data points.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Exactly to Ask For & Concrete Targets\n\n| Component | Current Offer | What to Ask For | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | The posted maximum is $271,000. You should not take a base salary pay cut coming from Meta ($240k). |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$50,000 \u2013 $75,000** | To compensate for leaving your upcoming Meta bonus and the next tranche of unvested equity ($200k+). |\n| **Equity (RSUs)** | $1,080,000 / 4 yrs | **$1,200,000 / 4 yrs** | To buffer against the front-loaded drop-off in Years 3 and 4. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Structure the Conversation\n\nHave a quick call with your recruiter and keep your tone appreciative, firm, and transparent:\n\n> *\"I\u2019m really impressed by the team and the scope of the role, and I appreciate the offer. Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and unvested equity at Meta, I took a close look at how this compares over a multi-year horizon.*\n>\n> *A few key gaps stand out:*\n> 1. *My current base is $240k, so $235k represents a step backward. Given the posted range tops out at $271k, I\u2019d like to see base salary at **$255k**.*\n> 2. *Leaving Meta means walking away from my upcoming bonus and near-term vesting shares ($200k in the next 12 months). A sign-on bonus of **$60k** would help make me whole on that transition.*\n> 3. *Because of Google's front-loaded vesting schedule, the back-half compensation drops considerably. Increasing the equity grant to **$1.2M** would give me long-term parity.*\n>\n> *If we can bridge these items, I\u2019d be excited to sign and lock this in.\"*\n\n### Final Takeaway\nEven if Google only moves on the **base salary (to ~$250k+)** and adds a **$40k\u2013$60k sign-on**, your Year 1 comp will approach **~$700k**, making the move financially compelling while you establish yourself for L6 refreshers.",
